  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "Which of these numbers is largest?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt{\\sqrt[3]{5\\cdot6}}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt{6\\sqrt[3]5}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt{5\\sqrt[3]6}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt[3]{5\\sqrt6}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt[3]{6\\sqrt5}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "All five choices are positive, so raising them to the sixth power preserves their order. The exponent \\(6\\) is useful because it removes both square roots and cube roots: for positive \\(t\\), these roots are \\(t^{1/2}\\) and \\(t^{1/3}\\).\n\nWrite \\(a,b,c,d,e\\) for the numbers in choices A through E. Their sixth powers are\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na^6&=5\\cdot6=30,\\\\\nb^6&=(6\\sqrt[3]5)^3=6^3\\cdot5=1080,\\\\\nc^6&=(5\\sqrt[3]6)^3=5^3\\cdot6=750,\\\\\nd^6&=(5\\sqrt6)^2=5^2\\cdot6=150,\\\\\ne^6&=(6\\sqrt5)^2=6^2\\cdot5=180.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(1080\\) is the largest of these numbers, the largest original value is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{6\\sqrt[3]5}}\\), choice B.",
